<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Books and Movies – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Books and Movies</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Liked Your Presentation Because...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;" colspan="3">
                <b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Give a brief overview of a favorite book or movie. Interactive: Participate in class discussions, sharing reviews of various types of content.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;" colspan="3">
                <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can give a brief overview of a favorite movie in class discussions (e.g., "My favorite movie is about space exploration").
                Can participate in class discussions by asking and answering questions about favorite books (e.g., "What book do you recommend?" "I recommend this book because it's exciting").
                Can share opinions in group discussions about favorite books (e.g., "I recommend reading this book because it is about friendship and adventure").
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key vocabulary and grammar for expressing preferences and reasons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various movie posters and book covers (e.g., a comedy, a fantasy book, a documentary). Students are asked to call out the <b>genre</b>. The teacher then asks, "What <b>genre</b> do you <b>prefer</b>? Why?" encouraging simple answers like "I <b>prefer</b> action movies."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences: "I like <b>watching</b> action movies because they are <b>thrilling</b>." "I <b>prefer</b> reading fantasy books over documentaries because they are more <b>engaging</b>." The teacher explicitly uses and points to keywords: <b>movie, book, genre, like, prefer, watching, reading, entertaining, engaging, emotional</b>. The teacher also models the correct pronunciation of present tense verbs ending in "-s" (e.g., "She *watches* ( /ɪz/ ) comedies," "He *reads* ( /z/ ) books").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I <b>prefer</b> comedies over documentaries,' do I like comedies more or less?" (Expected answer: More.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'comedy' an adjective or a <b>genre</b>?" (Expected answer: A <b>genre</b>.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Books and Movies" through an authentic dialogue.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (2-3 minutes) between two teenagers discussing their favorite books and movies. The dialogue incorporates the target grammar (present simple for preferences, past simple for previous experiences, comparing preferences) and vocabulary (<b>movie, book, character, plot, theme, genre, entertaining, engaging, emotional, enjoy, like, prefer</b>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue Snippet:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">A: "Hey, what kind of movies do you <b>enjoy</b>?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">B: "I generally <b>like watching</b> science fiction because they are often very <b>engaging</b>. What about you?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">A: "I <b>prefer</b> comedies over sci-fi because they make me <b>relax</b> and laugh. Yesterday, I <b>watched</b> a really funny one. It <b>was about</b> a group of friends on a crazy adventure; the <b>plot</b> was hilarious!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interacting orally to share opinions, preferences, and brief descriptions of content.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the dialogue twice. First, for gist:</p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main topic of the conversation?" (Expected answer: Favorite books and movies.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who likes comedies?" (Expected answer: Speaker A.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Second, for specific details (True/False):</p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Speaker B prefers documentaries." (False)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Speaker A recommends movies that are serious." (False)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking, using controlled and semi-controlled activities to practice target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present simple to describe preferences and reasons ("I like reading fiction books because they are exciting."), verb + -ing + noun + because, Past simple for brief descriptions ("I watched a comedy yesterday. It was about..."), Comparing preferences ("I prefer action movies over comedies because they are more thrilling.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> <b>movie, book, poetry, character, plot, theme, documentary, genre, express, describe, relax, watch, enjoy, like, prefer, entertaining, engaging, emotional, favorite</b>.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practicing /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ in third-person singular verbs and contrastive vowel sounds like /ʊ/ in "full" and /uː/ in "fool" through targeted listening and repetition if words containing these sounds arise. (e.g. "She *pulls* the book /pʊlz/", "The *fool* watches /fuːl/").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">
                        <b>1. Controlled Practice – Sentence Builders:</b> Students work individually to complete sentences using given vocabulary and grammar. The teacher provides sentence beginnings and word banks. E.g., "I like _______ (verb+ing) _______ (genre) books because they are _______ (adjective)." or "My <b>favorite</b> _______ (movie/book) is _______. It <b>was about</b> _______ (plot/theme)." Teacher monitors for correct use of verb + -ing, present/past simple, and appropriate vocabulary.
                        <br><i>(Example: "I like <b>reading</b> mystery books because they are <b>exciting</b>.")</i>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">
                        <b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Preference Statements:</b> In pairs, students use provided sentence frames to ask and answer questions about preferences. They rotate partners every 2 minutes.
                        <br><i>Student A asks:</i> "What kind of <b>movies</b> do you <b>prefer</b>?" or "Do you <b>enjoy watching documentaries</b>?"
                        <br><i>Student B answers:</i> "I <b>prefer</b> _______ (genre) over _______ (genre) because they are more _______ (adjective)." or "Yes, I <b>enjoy watching documentaries</b> because they are very <b>informative</b>."
                        <br>The teacher encourages using <b>verbs</b> like <b>express, describe, relax, watch, enjoy, analyze, reflect, like, prefer</b>. Emphasis is placed on clear pronunciation of "-s" endings for third-person singular and careful distinction of similar vowel sounds.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">
                        <b>3. Peer Check – Opinion Sharing:</b> Students form new pairs. Each student asks their partner about their "top 3 <b>favorite</b>" books or movies, focusing on giving reasons. They note down their partner's answers. Students then take turns sharing one of their partner's preferences with the class, e.g., "Juan <b>enjoys watching</b> horror movies because he thinks they are <b>thrilling</b>." Partners provide feedback on sentence structure and vocabulary use ("You said 'he enjoy', it should be 'he enjoys'").
                        <br><i>(Pragmatic: Participating in discussions, Giving opinions; Sociolinguistic: Providing constructive feedback.)</i>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario, focusing on fluency and interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Favorite Review" - Students work in groups of three. Each student prepares a brief (1-2 minute) oral overview of their <b>favorite</b> book or movie to "present" to their group. They must <b>describe</b> the <b>genre, plot, characters</b> (briefly), and most importantly, <b>express</b> why they <b>like</b> or <b>prefer</b> it, using reasons with "because."</p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example sentence starters: "My <b>favorite movie</b> is [Title]. It's a [<b>genre</b>] about [brief <b>plot/theme</b>]. I <b>like watching</b> it because it's very <b>entertaining</b>." "I <b>recommend reading</b> [Book Title] because it is about [<b>character</b> and adventure]."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They also use past simple to explain a past viewing/reading experience: "I <b>watched</b> it last month. It <b>was about</b> a young girl who saved the world."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Group Interaction:</b> After each student presents, the other two group members must provide constructive feedback, starting with the phrase, "I <b>liked</b> your <b>presentation</b> because..." (e.g., "I <b>liked</b> your <b>presentation</b> because you <b>described</b> the <b>plot</b> very clearly." "I <b>liked</b> your <b>presentation</b> because you made me want to <b>watch</b> that <b>movie</b>."). This directly addresses the theme and sociolinguistic competence. They can also ask 1-2 follow-up questions about the book/movie.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, using target language naturally to give opinions and compare preferences, and providing constructive feedback.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of students' speaking skills, followed by self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during Stage 4. The teacher circulates and notes students' use of target vocabulary, grammar, and interaction skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "My Favorite Review" presentations and engage in peer feedback and discussion within their groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary and verb endings (e.g., "-s," "-es," "-ies").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of target vocabulary (e.g., <b>genre, character, plot, entertaining, engaging, emotional, prefer</b>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of grammar (present simple for preferences, past simple for previous experience, verb + -ing + noun + because, comparing preferences).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in expressing preferences and reasons (using "because").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active participation in class discussions and providing constructive feedback using the theme phrase.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words or phrases did you use today to <b>express</b> your opinions about books and movies?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to explain *why* you <b>like</b> a book or movie? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson, informing future instructional planning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (Oral/Quick Write):</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you do well today when talking about your <b>favorite</b> books or movies?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you need to practice more to <b>express</b> your opinions clearly about books and movies?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the speaking activities (e.g., 'Preference Statements,' 'My Favorite Review') worked particularly well for students at A2.3 level? Did they manage to provide constructive feedback effectively?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were there any specific vocabulary items (e.g., <b>plot, character, theme</b>) or grammar points (e.g., verb + -ing, past simple usage) that students struggled with in their speaking? How can I scaffold this for the next lesson or review it in future lessons?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I adjust the next lesson (Writing) to build on today's speaking practice effectively? (e.g., turning their oral reviews into written paragraphs).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> Today's speaking practice on giving overviews and reviews of books and movies, along with expressing opinions and reasons, will directly prepare students for Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), where they will write a short review or recommendation for their favorite book or movie, incorporating the same vocabulary and grammatical structures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>